\newlabel{fig:sign_norm_removed_cel}{{11}{18}{Sign-balance normalization ablation for the unknown/complex-removed \textit {C.\ elegans} magnitude pattern after removing the original signs. Scaling by the original spectral radius weakens the performance increase associated with changing the negative edge fraction}{figure.11}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces  Performance--CV islands for the shuffle controls, shown with 50\% density contours. Columns correspond to GR, IPC, KR, and MC. Rows show all-positive binary controls, sign-preserving signed-unit controls, and real-valued \textit  {C.\ elegans} controls. Contour color shows the percent change in raw spectral radius relative to the row baseline, \(\Delta \rho _{\mathrm  {raw}}\); blue indicates lower raw spectral radius and red indicates higher raw spectral radius.As a control, we normalize by the spectral radius of the baseline matrix rather than by the post-ablation spectral radius of \(W^{\mathrm  {res}}\), allowing us to isolate effects of the ablations that are not driven solely by changes in spectral radius. }}{19}{figure.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:shuf_all}{{12}{19}{Performance--CV islands for the shuffle controls, shown with 50\% density contours. Columns correspond to GR, IPC, KR, and MC. Rows show all-positive binary controls, sign-preserving signed-unit controls, and real-valued \textit {C.\ elegans} controls. Contour color shows the percent change in raw spectral radius relative to the row baseline, \(\Delta \rho _{\mathrm {raw}}\); blue indicates lower raw spectral radius and red indicates higher raw spectral radius.As a control, we normalize by the spectral radius of the baseline matrix rather than by the post-ablation spectral radius of \(W^{\mathrm {res}}\), allowing us to isolate effects of the ablations that are not driven solely by changes in spectral radius}{figure.12}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces Network visualizations for architectures A--H. In all panels, nodes represent neurons, orange edges are excitatory synapses, blue edges are inhibitory synapses, and darker edges indicate stronger synaptic weights. A: original \emph  {C.~elegans} connectome. B: original connectome with shuffled weights. C: connection-shuffled \emph  {C.~elegans} connectome. D: connection-shuffled connectome with an additional weight shuffle. E: original topology with signed binary ($\pm 1$) weights determined by the original edge signs. F: panel E after sign shuffling. G: connection-shuffled version of panel E. H: panel G after sign shuffling.}}{19}{figure.13}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces Additional control architectures I--O. I: \emph  {C.~elegans} connectome with binary weights. J: binary-weight \emph  {C.~elegans} connectome after connection shuffling. K: \emph  {C.~elegans} topology with Gaussian weights. L: \emph  {C.~elegans}-matched Erd\H {o}s--R\'enyi network with Gaussian weights. M: small-world network with $p = 0.1$ and Gaussian weights. N: sign-preserving Gaussian model. O: sign-preserving uniform model.}}{20}{figure.14}\protected@file@percent }
